  i've spoken to multiple people who have  actually left the police force because  of these things people aren't shocked  with the the news that's come out and  and now that i i know these things since  the vigil um i have no trust in the  police force whatsoever  is that something that was changed  because of your experience did you have  trust in the police force beforehand i  did before yeah i've never had an issue  with the police before but again that's  because i'm white and from a certain  background and  i just wouldn't be a main target for  police  what was going through your mind when  you were being taken away by police we  saw the shots of you in handcuffs and  that that picture that that many people  now used to to mark the sarah everard  vigil what was going through your mind  um i thought that i would get kicked out  of university i thought i'd never be  able to work again um you know i didn't  expect that to happen like i said i've  never been in trouble with the police  even on a small scale so i had no idea  what was happening um i've said before i  was terrified a lot of people don't  believe me because the way i looked in  those videos but at the time you don't  realize what is going through someone's  head and the processing of that um you  know it was traumatizing i had  nightmares afterwards it was awful  the met claim that they've got a record  number of uh female officers and and  certainly crested dick  claimed that moves were being made to  change the the culture of the the police  force why do you think that in reality  it seems that that nothing has changed  the problem is that a lot of people do  say that if you've hired more women  police officers then that's going to  change things that doesn't change  anything at all women can still have  internalized misogyny they can still be  racist and homophobic and transphobic so  to say that they might hire more police  who are women um you know to have you've  got  who was the head of the police a gay  woman so and and she didn't do a good  job it doesn't matter if you're a woman  or a man if you are internal internally  misogynistic and not  holding accountable the issues that are  going on within your police force it  doesn't matter what gender you are it  doesn't matter what sex you are it  doesn't matter who you are as a person  you need to have those belief systems  that the police are there to protect and  serve the public no matter what  do you believe though that this will  make it more difficult for another woman  to step forward um and take on this role  again i don't think it matters if a  woman or a man steps forward by like  they need to be someone who understands  what is going on systemically within the  police force and i don't know whether  that means that hopefully we have  someone from the outside even it just  needs to be someone who is unbiased and  will not let police officers get away  with this kind of behaviour  do you think that there does now need to  be further investigation into the  culture that has come to the fall that  exists within the police force there  always should have been um you know the  countless um independent investigations  that they've had you've got the police  investigating the police here and things  like this although they will come to  like a few things that they've done  wrong it needs to be ongoing throughout  the whole of the force it's not even  just the met police it's all police um  you know there really needs to be a  proper investigation on how to radically  change the systemic issues  patty thank you so much for speaking to  us uh this evening thank you for sharing  your views with us patsy stevenson there
 

